 2. Altman confident AI industry can handle risks 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Altman talks with Benioff yesterday at Dreamforce in San Francisco. Photo: Ina Fried/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 OpenAI CEO Sam Altman told Dreamforce yesterday that while people were right to be afraid of the risks of AI, he was confident that the industry would be able to develop the technology safely.

 Why it matters: Altman, along with other AI leaders, has echoed Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei's recent call for slowing the pace of AI development.

 Driving the news: Speaking at Salesforce's conference in San Francisco, Altman said that "some degree of accidents are unavoidable with new technology across the industry."

 He noted how safe air travel has become as a result of accident reporting and learning and regulation by federal agencies. Companies should be prepared "to slow down or stop if we get to a point where we can't" proceed safely, Altman said. The big picture: AI has rapidly advanced from barely conversational chatbots to systems that can transform enterprise work, write complex software and solve difficult mathematical problems — making once-abstract risks feel increasingly immediate.

 "The world is right to be afraid of this," Altman told the conference. Between the lines: Altman pointed to social media as a cautionary example. He said social platforms have produced real benefits, but their effects on society — particularly young people — have not been "only positive."

 "I don't think short-form video is inherently evil, but I do think it's dangerous," Altman said, adding that responsibility is shared among parents, companies and governments. Altman noted that he likes to unwind with a few such videos, but said he would not let his kids watch them. Zoom out: Likening AI technology to getting "fire from the gods," the OpenAI CEO said that the world is on the precipice of a third wave, after chatbots and coding agents, in which AI can proactively carry out work on people's behalf.

 "We have only scratched the surface of figuring out how to build great products that really make people more capable, more powerful." Yes, but: He noted that there is a lot of inertia in human work and that it will take years for the technology to be fully distributed.

 
 
