 3. From obscure philosophy to AI bogeyman 
 
 By Herb Scribner 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Trump speaks to reporters before boarding Air Force One last week. Photo: Andrew Harnik/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Effective altruism, or EA, has helped shape (and fund) parts of the AI safety movement.

 Why it matters: The philosophy this week became the Trump administration's newest villain in the battle over AI development .

 State of play: The Trump administration criticized AI leaders who called for a slower development pace due to concerns about catastrophic risks from the technology.

 President Trump bashed AI doomers on social media, claiming that fears of AI are a hoax on par with "Russiagate" and the president's own impeachments. The Defense Department called out the EA movement by name, posting "Americanism, not effective altruism" on X, and Defense Department Under Secretary Emil Michael told Fox News that EA and the push for AI safety "comes from a deep-rooted ideology that is pretty dystopian." How it works: Effective altruism urges the use of data to determine how time and money can do the most good for humanity.

 The movement took off in the 2010s through universities, philanthropy and online communities with a focus on causes like malaria prevention and farm-animal welfare. It soon became influential within Silicon Valley. More recently, AI safety has become a major focus for some parts of the EA movement , particularly among "longtermists," who believe that protecting future generations from catastrophe should be a top priority.

 Between the lines: EA-linked groups have been spending on AI alignment and safety for years, with the goal of making sure that humans don't lose control of AI and that the technology doesn't lead to catastrophic events.

 Open Philanthropy, an EA-linked organization now known as Coefficient Giving, donated $30 million to OpenAI back in 2017. Time reported deep EA ties among Anthropic's early staff and funders, though neither CEO Dario Amodei nor president Daniela Amodei has identified as an effective altruist. The other side: The Trump administration argues that the AI safety worldview associated with EA would put America at risk of falling behind China in the AI race.

